Here is a Graphviz DOT schematic showing each hyperparameter configuration evaluated across the folds, with fold scores averaged before selecting the best configuration:

```dot
digraph cv_tuning {
  rankdir=LR;
  graph [bgcolor="white", pad="0.25", nodesep="0.35", ranksep="0.55"];
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Arial", fontsize=10];
  edge [fontname="Arial", fontsize=9, color="#546E7A"];

  data  [label="Dataset", fillcolor="#FFF3B0"];
  grid  [label="Hyperparameter\ncandidates", fillcolor="#FFD6A5"];
  cv    [label="Evaluate each candidate\nwith K-fold CV", shape=oval,
         fillcolor="#BDE0FE"];
  mean  [label="Average the K\nvalidation scores", fillcolor="#CDEAC0"];
  best  [label="Select candidate with\nbest mean score", shape=diamond,
         fillcolor="#E4C1F9"];
  final [label="Refit on all training data\nusing selected parameters",
         fillcolor="#FFC8DD"];

  data -> cv;
  grid -> cv;

  subgraph cluster_folds {
    label="One candidate: repeat for folds 1 … K";
    style="rounded,filled";
    color="#A0C4FF";
    fillcolor="#F7FAFF";

    train [label="Train on K−1 folds", fillcolor="#CDEAC0"];
    valid [label="Validate on held-out fold", fillcolor="#FFADAD"];
    score [label="Record fold score", fillcolor="#BDE0FE"];

    train -> valid [label="evaluate"];
    valid -> score;
  }

  cv -> train [style=dashed];
  score -> mean;
  mean -> best -> final;
}
```

Render it with `dot -Tpng kfold_tuning.dot -o kfold_tuning.png`.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to the requested answer: it provides a direct, colorful DOT script and describes the basic process accurately. Its main limitation is that it depicts a single hyperparameter set and does not clearly show that the evaluation-and-aggregation process is repeated for multiple candidate configurations. The fold scores also connect to the aggregate node via validation nodes rather than explicit score nodes.

Assistant B provides extensive Python code that can generate a colorful diagram, but its process is misleading for ordinary hyperparameter tuning. It places hyperparameter tuning inside each fold’s training data, then aggregates those fold results to select one configuration. That describes something closer to nested evaluation, but the diagram does not show an inner CV/search process, and choosing one global configuration from fold-specific tuning is not clearly justified. It also calls the held-out fold a “testing set,” which should generally be called a validation fold in this tuning workflow. Its claim that the resulting evaluation is unbiased is too strong: using CV scores to select the best configuration can introduce selection bias; an independent test set or properly designed nested CV is needed for an unbiased performance estimate.

Both answers could make the repetition across hyperparameter candidates clearer. Overall, A is more concise and less misleading, although it omits an important part of the tuning loop.

My final verdict is: [[A>B]]

