    train1 -> valid1 [label="score"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_fold2 {
    label="Fold 2";
    style=filled; color="#BDE0FE";
    train2 [label="Train on folds 1 & 3", fillcolor="#B7E4C7"];
    valid2 [label="Validate on fold 2", fillcolor="#FFADAD"];
    train2 -> valid2 [label="score"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_fold3 {
    label="Fold 3";
    style=filled; color="#FFD6A5";
    train3 [label="Train on folds 1–2", fillcolor="#B7E4C7"];
    valid3 [label="Validate on fold 3", fillcolor="#FFADAD"];
    train3 -> valid3 [label="score"];
  }

  valid1 -> mean;
  valid2 -> mean;
  valid3 -> mean;
  mean -> best;
  best -> refit;
  refit -> test;
}
```

Save it as `kfold_tuning.dot` and render it with `dot -Tpng kfold_tuning.dot -o kfold_tuning.png`.

### Evaluation

Both answers provide a colorful Graphviz-based schematic and are relevant to the request. The key distinction is how well they represent hyperparameter tuning.

Assistant A includes a hyperparameter search space and an explicit selection step, but its depicted procedure is misleading: it tunes on each fold’s training portion, evaluates on that fold’s held-out portion, and then selects hyperparameters using those held-out scores. Using the same scores both to choose hyperparameters and to claim an unbiased performance estimate can produce optimistic results. A proper standard CV tuning diagram would evaluate each fixed candidate across the folds, aggregate its validation scores, and then select the best candidate. Alternatively, nested CV is needed for an unbiased estimate after tuning. A is also substantially more verbose than necessary.

Assistant B is concise and gives usable DOT code directly. Its fold splits and aggregation broadly illustrate the CV mechanics, but the diagram shows only “Hyperparameter Set A,” so it does not actually depict comparing multiple candidate configurations. The final “select best” step therefore lacks an explicit search-and-repeat process. Still, B’s depiction is closer to the standard practice of evaluating a fixed configuration across folds and aggregating results, and it avoids A’s more consequential ambiguity about using held-out fold scores for both tuning and evaluation.

Overall, B is slightly better, though an ideal answer would make the candidate-configuration loop explicit and distinguish validation-based selection from final evaluation on a separate test set.

[[B>A]]

